#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167896} :activity/id #uuid "797cab7f-b62f-590f-9f20-4093fac9d314" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167991 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of ordinary differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is a forcing vector.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll derive the formula for the particular solution of the above system using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_c(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a vector of constants, and $\\Phi$ is the fundamental matrix.\n\nAs usual, for the method of variation of parameters, we'll look for a particular solution of our inhomogeneous system by varying the constant parameters, i.e., in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined.\n\nDifferentiating $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φ\\,u(t))^{′} \\\\  & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a solution, substituting it back into the initial system gives a true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Substituting this into the equation simplifies it to the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+AΦu(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t) & =f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\Phi$ is invertible, we can pre-multiply both sides by $\\Phi^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate both sides of the final equations (ignoring the constant term):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\displaystyle\\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99630576-f78f-593a-af83-87c98421e2c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14396 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "94760db8-7585-5363-8838-c0f44735b806" :step/math-academy-id 592305 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166524 :question/id #uuid "6060e2fe-05b3-587d-b5dd-3cdab705eb05" :question/math-academy-id "e-21546"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483418}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "4a088561-5bc3-556f-b5d5-470901c2c1cf" :step/math-academy-id 592306 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166525 :question/id #uuid "1d203940-c05d-5f30-a8ad-c618d6d6ba5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497352}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "387d38d2-d1da-59ba-b0d2-529bed76253e" :step/math-academy-id 592309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166526 :question/id #uuid "ce663953-3e5e-55e2-a8db-6d7a531496cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497377}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "9a3cf6ac-30bf-5301-9c2b-c2870c934a65" :step/math-academy-id 592312}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166985}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497449} :activity/id #uuid "b003c2d7-68ae-5238-bc0f-46f982a5621d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e43401-278c-58f1-b818-fad8546bda0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303797} :answer-field/id #uuid "295a6ea8-2810-531b-90a1-1e521e6ec6e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596f4cf7-f992-59e0-a1ab-314f9b22d6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303002} {:answer/id #uuid "5e995f6d-38bc-503f-add8-819490663c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496865} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a0f445-2c98-5d5e-9321-6d979dc294b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc4c9e61-81e1-5e97-95cb-a89bd2a71a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-330899" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-6{e}^{2t})-5{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 12{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "263c0b72-c299-51ac-98e0-f88dd4d823d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08055b09-dc8e-5e64-a169-6b8646c97e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18t-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302508} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e093a4e-16c7-597e-9369-a2860151d355" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6dc5997-1d4e-5312-9245-798d648973c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-329817" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-12t-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 8t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=8t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=8\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =8t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}+\\int 8\\,{e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}-8{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-t}(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}(1+t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t)-16(1+t) \\\\ 2(1-2t)-8(1+t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18t-15 \\\\ -12t-6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7c983da7-ca72-5a73-bf3a-e7f56ec9519d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2cccf5-489b-5e70-b3a9-9d2661562299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365794} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7d0200-75d6-56dc-9a3b-832b6fae8337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496829} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef783e3c-2210-5313-90c8-cc217b3c7be3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "974ddd49-be50-5576-a845-339cab6a2a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365796} {:answer/id #uuid "0aacdf65-443f-56da-b835-155b66a57197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496676} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4dc56ff-d11b-5257-828e-dfd7f038bf79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cab04d20-21da-5031-a5e8-e08eadd456c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336698" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(-2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "842ea0d1-eabb-5f2a-bca0-30802bda115c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee041e3a-2f24-52a5-bb75-0aca1b12f5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497167}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497167} :answer-field/id #uuid "257f025c-7aa8-5698-b6e8-90af2e776c37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5163d8a-75af-5737-80f1-4aea32343cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497060}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497060} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e897fb2-9841-5b9d-a780-0a38ba920dc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cbf4a14-013f-525c-b169-07b2a7a14f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-337102" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2{e}^{3t}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{10}{3}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}{e}^{t}-0\\cdot {e}^{t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -2{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 4{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "98dc1837-552f-563e-a960-0f664d971bb5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4c6396-6267-59ce-81e1-3b70290d6465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80t+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496717} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbf6a85-16be-5ce4-a166-d683ed32df25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8720bb82-7e04-58ea-82b1-0eeb9a80a59b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337285" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$90t+45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 15t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-5t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 15t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -5t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 15t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-5t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-5\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -5t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-5t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-5\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{5}{2}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=15t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=15\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 15t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =15t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 15\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-15t{e}^{-t}+\\int 15{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-15t{e}^{-t}-15{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =-15{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -15{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}10(1-2t)-60(t+1) \\\\ 15(1-2t)-60(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}80t+50 \\\\ 90t+45\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc7dda2f-cd2a-50cf-8f0a-dcfa59501811"}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496656}]}
